@meta
id: 06-14
title: Do that again with semicolon
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: repeat-semicolon, find-f
par: 28
minutes: 4
vscode: four clicks, four spacebars
open: refund.args.ts

@brief
Someone has committed this type with the formatter switched off. Every colon
has lost the space that follows it:

    { orderId: string, amount: number, reason: string, channel: string }

Put the four spaces back. The targets are identical — the next colon, then
the next, then the next — which is a pattern worth having a key for rather
than typing `f:` over and over.

@keys
| `f:` | forward to the next colon |
| `;` | repeat the last `f`, `F`, `t` or `T` |
| `a` | insert to the right of the cursor |

@hints
Do the first colon with `f:` and an insert. Then notice that the next three
jobs are the same job.
--
`;` repeats whatever find you last performed, in the same direction, from
wherever the cursor is now. After `f:`, every `;` is another `f:`.
--
`f:` `a` space `Esc`, then `;` `a` space `Esc` three more times.

@solution
    f : a  <Esc> ; a  <Esc> ; a  <Esc> ; a  <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The first jump costs two keys and every jump after it costs one. That is the
trade `;` makes: you describe the target once and then repeat the
description as often as you like.

Two details that decide whether this works. Insert with `a` rather than `i`,
so the cursor ends up past the colon — if it ended before the colon, the
next `;` would find the very same colon again and you would stack up two
spaces. And `;` repeats the last find in the whole editor, not the last find
on this line, so it keeps working after you move down a line, which is what
makes the drills in this chapter flow.

`,` repeats the same find in the opposite direction, and that is next.

@start refund.args.ts
export type RefundArgs = { orderId:string, amount:number, reason:string, channel:string };

@expected refund.args.ts
export type RefundArgs = { orderId: string, amount: number, reason: string, channel: string };
